[Music]  skin lightening is about power  it's an expression of power and so  people who lighten their skin are doing  this because they are responding to  a power dynamic that exists in their  particular society  in many parts of the world lighter skin  is considered to be more beautiful than  darker skin  a beauty standard that's been around for  hundreds of years  lighter skin has been associated with  power and privilege and the beauty  industry has been profiting  selling skin lightening creams and soaps  which are popular across asia africa and  the caribbean  by 2027 the global market for skin  lighteners is estimated to reach over 12  billion dollars  skin whitening is definitely a huge  business  the patients look forward to have a much  fairer or a whiter skin and they could  go to any length to achieve that  [Music]  [Music]  the history  of skin lightening here in the united  states it actually begins with white  women white women here would use skin  lightening products because they were  trying to emulate  beauty standards and beauty practices  that were circulating in europe  and then around the jim crow era what we  see is skin lightening products  being used and sold and circulated  within the african-american community  black women would use this partly  because  it would be  a survival strategy essentially  the lighter your skin was the more  access to resources you would have and  so  this is what  made skin lightening products in part  popular  among more communities of color  the idea was that if you were trying to  emulate the people in power  you were seen as more civilized you were  seen as cleaner  and therefore that would give you access  to certain things it would allow you  upward mobility upward class mobility  etc  when i was a kid i could say i was one  shade  more darker than my sister  and we both went to the same school  everybody used to be a little surprised  when  i told them she was my sister so they  said how is there such a huge difference  in your skin color  so i always had this but there was no  pressure from my family members  but friends and classmates definitely  did question me about my skin color once  upon a time  in asia the practice of skin whitening  is related to colorism a looks-based  discrimination that occurs within racial  or ethnic groups  and in india a part of everyday life  individuals who have  fairer skin are more acceptable  when it comes to career or marriage  if you also go to the matrimonial  columns in the newspapers or magazines  they say i look for a fairer bride or  there are  women who look for a fairer groom so  it's equal  desire to have a fairer skin is deep  seated in indian population  most experts agree that colorism bled  into india's caste system where darker  skin has since become associated with  indians at the lowest levels of the  social hierarchy who do manual labor  historians are undecided about the  origins of this colour prejudice  while some point to ancient migrations  by indo-europeans arabs and persians all  of whom had strikingly lighter coloured  skin than local inhabitants  others note the more recent colonial  rule of the british empire  the britishers ruled us for about  roughly 200 years the indians were kind  of treated  very badly or they were slaves they  worked for the britishers for many many  years so they look forward to the  british skin and they thought that the  british skin was acceptable because they  were fairer asia is the biggest market  for skin lightening products the world  health organization found that 40 of  women they asked in china india and  other asian countries said they  regularly use them  in india skin lightening is a  multi-million dollar industry around 80  of skin moisturizers on the market are  for skin whitening  the most widely used was fair and lovely  it earns more than 500 million dollars  each year for unilever from india alone  so i used to see the product fair and  lovely even while i was a kid so it's  about  45 years old  it is quite popular and it's  manufactured by a company called  hindustan unilever limited and  it's a booming business  due to global public pressure on brands  following the 2020 george floyd protests  the indian affiliate of unilever changed  the name to glow and lovely  in a statement the company said we  recognize the use of words fair white  and light suggests a singular ideal of  beauty that we don't think is right and  we want to address this  the indian government has proposed jail  terms and fines for advertisements which  promote skin whitening  but that hasn't stopped many indians  obsessed with lighter skin buying and  using these products  you know something like this has been  going on in our societies for so long  we discriminate people based on their  skin colon and no one's saying anything  about it you know when you see the way  it affects children it affects women it  affects men  and they grow with that sense of  insecurity  it's like a journey we've been on this  toxic belief you know where it started  with culture it started with social  hierarchies and the media keeps  endorsing it  companies they may not have initiated it  but by endorsing it by selling products  through advertisements that say that you  know you can be whiter in seven days or  or white is better if you're fairer  you'll be more successful too the thing  is they're using the insecurity that  colorism has bred to sell their product  so you're kind of making money off of  people's insecurities  and then you know we just lived with it  and it's become normal  apart from using whitening products some  people go even further receiving  cosmetic procedures like chemical peels  or laser treatment  in recent years an antioxidant called  glutathione is gaining popularity  while it's available to take orally many  consumers believe injecting the drug is  faster and more effective for skin  whitening but this hasn't been  scientifically proven  if they have a medical condition  yes i would do it but if they just want  to achieve a skin whitening  you know treatment i would refuse to do  that for them  because uh the safety and efficacy of  the drug  glutathione is not well established  glutathione is produced naturally in the  human liver in india oral glutathione is  approved for treating skin conditions  and for skin whitening but intravenous  or intramuscular glutathione is only  approved to treat medical conditions  like the side effects of chemotherapy  there are very few clinical trials there  are certain drawbacks to those studies  because uh those studies were done on a  very limited number of subjects also the  patients were not followed up for a long  term or a long period  there are practitioners who do use it  but it's an off label indication  but what has been observed is that  it's a very subjective opinion from the  patient in terms of  how much of a skin  lightening they have been able to  achieve with the treatment so it's not  necessary that if you administer the  infusion in 10 patients all of the 10  patients are going to observe the same  change  due to a lack of research and  regulations clinics make up their own  protocols and set their own prices  ranging from 50 to 70 dollars per  injection  in the philippines almost 400 years of  spanish and then american colonial rule  helped to reinforce a notion of beauty  as white and so today glutathione is  becoming a popular alternative to  traditional lightning products  when we look at a place like the  philippines  people there will use skin lightening  products because they're trying to  emulate  those who are in power they're trying to  emulate things that they see on tv  and when you watch for example  philippine tv it's inundated with actors  and actresses and performers who  are typically light-skinned when you  look at the philippine government many  politicians are either mixed race or  light skinned  based on my experience  owns a beauty salon in downtown manila  which offers beauty services including  glutathione drips for skin whitening  some of my  my interviews  lighter young skin nila so talaganga  the country's food and drug  administration has warned against the  use of injectable glutathione for skin  whitening flagging its toxic effects on  the liver kidneys and nervous system  but many big medical groups and small  beauty salons still provide it with a  price tag of up to 120 dollars per  session  [Music]  some beauty salons or moonlighting  nurses even offer injections at home  but glutathione drips can also be bought  easily online so some take it into their  own hands following online tutorials  so for tonight's video i'm gonna show  you how to inject yourself with  glutathione mm-hmm  i would say that is unacceptable because  sometimes if it is injected in the wrong  manner or if it is injected in a huge  dose it can cause to severe allergic  reactions sometimes it there can be a  casualty or an emergency  these videos should be  totally prohibited on social media  platforms  i think it's an issue of access the more  available  a certain process is the more people are  going to be curious about it  these more advanced  processes what they do is they create  more legitimacy for the practice  but at no point do we step back and say  well why is this necessary to begin with  while some are still doing whatever it  takes to become lighter others are  beginning to embrace their own skin and  awareness of color discrimination has  increased in recent years  in india advocacy group darkest  beautiful has been organizing workshops  and events to fight colorism what needs  to happen is change both within culture  as well as change from you know these  external endorsements and and cosmetic  brands and businesses have a  responsibility you know it's not about  people want it and so we do it  this is not like some a problem that we  can fix overnight it takes several such  movements several 